I'm curious as to why low-altar games are hard as the game stands
right now.  You're guaranteed one in minetown and if you need to stop
off and b/u/c identify items there's one halfway (valley) and three
quarters down (Orcus' town).  Some quests have one too, so that's
another a quarter of the way down.  Early holy water might be a
problem if the altar isn't co-aligned, but that's not necessarily a
game-breaker in the early game (in my opinion).

Though, you talking about getting Sting on the first offering makes me
think that this approach makes it more useful to name Sting and
Orcrist before sacrificing if you are chaotic.  I hadn't thought of
that.
 
> This also seems to really unbalance things in favor of classes that can
> dip for excalibur.  Or have guaranteed first artifacts that are useful (W,
> V).

I would consider those the "easy" classes anyway.  Perhaps it's just
my personal take, but I like this because it gives more
differentiation between the classes.  If you could play every class
and always end up with <insert my favorite blessed fooproof +7
artifact here>, then every class ends up being (mostly) the same.  If
the game coerces you (even though you can always wish) to play with
different artifacts for different classes it only adds to class
differentiation.  I consider this a good thing, not an unbalancing
thing.

Or, perhaps what I'm really saying is that I /don't/ think the classes
should be balanced and that some should be much easier and some much
more of a challenge.

